A1. This may follow the swallow. ~ DYSPEPSIA
A8. One reason these got into the ring was because they were so smooth. ~ OPALS
A12. This means a lot of trouble. ~ ADO
A13. A general who lived long before Gen. Motors was heard of. ~ LEE
A14. The time, the place and the girl (plur.). ~ DATES
A16. Another case of we. ~ US
A17. The kind of tender that is not legal in the U. S. A. ~ BAR
A18. A character in Norse mythology. ~ ODIN
A19. This means to really live. ~ BE
A20. Major Operation often follows up this kind of trouble. ~ ORGANIC
A22. This was drunk on New Year's Day. ~ ALE
A23. The kind of a hat that is not felt in hot weather. ~ STRAW
A27. To give off. ~ EMIT
A28. Where social climbers may land. ~ ATOP
A29. What the stars can do now. ~ EAT
A30. What some member of a bridge foursome always does. ~ GABS
A31. Here is where the rub comes. ~ SPOTS
A32. The time the wise old owl keeps still. ~ DAY
A33. A dinner affair. ~ MENU
A34. A hesitating expression. ~ AHEM
A35. A conjunction. ~ OR
A36. This makes a lot of noise and that is all it is good for. ~ RATTLEBOX
A38. You will find all sorts of types here. ~ PI
A39. The outgrowth of the little stone church in the vale. ~ MOSS
A40. A turning point. ~ AXLE
A41. This will never be lost. ~ WON
A42. The way the wets feel now. ~ MOIST
A44. The first hazard. ~ ANTE
A45. This was very dear in war-time. ~ PAL
A46. The kind of men that are turned out of big industrial plants. ~ IDLE
A47. This is always starting stories. ~ ONCE
A48. These often try some monkey business. ~ DAGOS
A50. A corn producer. ~ TOE
A51. A back number in automobiles. ~ LICENSE
A53. You have an alternative here. ~ OR
A54. The kind of a bone they had to pick. ~ WISH
A55. The end of the Kingdom (Beersheba was at the other end). ~ DAN
A56. How the Constitution started. ~ WE
A57. What a relief! ~ CAMEO
A58. Little things in a printer's life. ~ EMS
A59. One of the first things to come out in the spring. ~ HOE
A60. To long for (not for anyone if the present styles keep up). ~ YEARN
A61. The hot season in Mexico. ~ REDPEPPER

D1. What some people call modern art. ~ DAUBS
D2. Measures adopted by certain football teams (abbr.). ~ YDS
D3. One way to get in solid (before the lid is on). ~ SO
D4. There is always a crook in this joint. ~ ELBOW
D5. This sounds like two and tastes like more. ~ PEAR
D6. This has uniform habits. ~ SERGEANT
D7. This is what they call a beautiful man. ~ ADONIS
D8. Pertaining to the ear. ~ OTIC
D9. A popular writer. ~ PEN
D10. This is always found in hash. ~ AS
D11. You will have to study hard to get this one. ~ SHEEPSKIN
D15. This might let you into a gold mine. ~ ADIT
D19. She signed on the dotted line and then came this. ~ BLOT
D21. The poor pedestrian might get the right of way in this. ~ AMBULANCE
D22. A little matter. ~ ATOM
D24. This could be a drop of glycerin. ~ TEAR
D25. Just a little sunshine. ~ RAY
D26. The rear of the flat. ~ AT
D28. Here is a tip on that triangle affair. ~ APEX
D30. He that has does this. ~ GETS
D31. This started out as a vamp without any sole, but after being reformed it now has a perfectly good mate. ~ SHOE
D32. Here is where they used to run around in the gay nighties. ~ DORMITORY
D33. This is always very upright. ~ MAST
D34. This describes Abraham Lincoln in more ways than one. ~ ABLE
D36. A well known American beauty. ~ ROSE
D37. What has been done to the hemline. ~ EXTENDED
D38. A sportsman made a big hit in this. ~ POLO
D39. This can be done in a minute. ~ MILE
D41. There is a lot of humor in this. ~ WAG
D43. Something sweet about a modest violet. ~ ODOR
D44. This was dropped from the navy. ~ ANCHOR
D45. A household necessity. ~ PA
D47. A river in France. ~ OISE
D48. Having a head of solid ivory. ~ DENSE
D49. You will have to do this if you want to go straight. ~ STEER
D51. To draw (that doesn't mean to draw a check or a breath). ~ LIMN
D52. Coarse hominy. ~ SAMP
D54. "It ain't going to reign no more." ~ WAR
D56. A lot of grief. ~ WOE
D57. This might stand for canned applesauce. (But some men never will). ~ CA
D59. A hot potato (abbr.). ~ HP


